.TH VELTROSRV 4
.SH NAME
veltrosrv \- the Veltro agent harness, served as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B veltrosrv
[
.B -v
] [
.B -n
.I maxsteps
] [
.B -m
.I toolmount
] [
.B -S
.I scratchdir
] [
.B -a
.I tag
] [
.B -t
.IR tool , ...
] [
.B -p
.IR path [: ro | rw ], ...
] [
.B -M
.I mountpoint
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Veltrosrv
runs the Veltro agent loop (the model at
.BR /mnt/llm ,
the tools at
.BR /tool )
and serves it as a filesystem, by default at
.BR /mnt/veltro .
Every front end is a client of these files:
.IR veltro (1)
on the command line,
.I lucibridge
for Lucia, Xenith's
.B Agent
window, or a shell with
.I echo
and
.IR cat .
.PP
The server mounts itself in the namespace of the process that starts it
and returns.  The serving process first forks its own namespace and
applies
.IR nsconstruct 's
restriction with the grants given by
.B -t
and
.BR -p ,
so the agent runs with those and nothing else; the mount lands only in
the caller's namespace, so the agent cannot name its own
.B ctl
or
.B approve
files.  Different grants are different servers, mounted at the same
name in different namespaces.
.PP
The options:
.TP
.B -v
log the trajectory to standard error as well as to each session's
.B log
file.
.TP
.BI -n " maxsteps"
the most model steps one turn may take (default 100).
.TP
.BI -m " toolmount"
the tool server's mount (default
.BR /tool ;
a Lucia activity uses
.BI /tool. n ).
.TP
.BI -S " scratchdir"
where tool output too large for the model is written; the agent is told
the path it sees that directory at,
.BR /tmp/veltro/scratch .
.TP
.BI -a " tag"
prefixed to every provenance record, for example
.BR activity=0 .
.TP
.BI -t " tool,..."
make the tool server's active set exactly these tools.
.TP
.BI -p " path,..."
register each path with the tool server and grant it to the agent.  A
.B :ro
or
.B :rw
suffix sets the permission.
.TP
.BI -M " mountpoint"
serve at another name.
.SH FILES
.TF approve
.TP
.B new
read: start a session and return its id, a small integer.
.TP
.BI id /ctl
write, one request per write:
.RS
.TP
.B cancel
stop the running turn at its next step; a call waiting on
.B approve
is denied.
.TP
.B reset
start a new model session for this conversation.
.TP
.B close
end the session.
.TP
.BI persona " type"
layer
.BI /lib/veltro/agents/ type .txt
into the system prompt as the agent's role.
.TP
.BI role " name"
append
.B /lib/veltro/meta.txt
.RB ( meta )
or
.BI /lib/veltro/agents/ name .txt
to the system prompt, as Lucia's activities do.
.TP
.BI brief " text"
append
.I text
to the system prompt.
.TP
.BI model " name"
.TP
.BI think " tokens"
.TP
.BI maxsteps " n"
.TP
.BR gate " on | off"
whether destructive calls wait on
.B approve
(default on).
.RE
.IP
Read: the settings, as
.IR attr = value
pairs on one line.
.TP
.BI id /input
write: one user message.  It starts a turn; while one runs the write
fails with
.BR busy .
.TP
.BI id /text
read: the conversation as the user sees it, as messages each headed by
a line
.BR "== user" ,
.B "== veltro"
or
.BR "== note" ,
the last optionally followed by a title.  A line of the model's own text
that would read as a header is given a leading space.  While a turn
runs, a read at the end waits for more, so the model's text arrives as
it is generated; when the session is idle it is the end of the file.
.I Cat
therefore prints the conversation and returns when the turn is over.
.TP
.BI id /log
read: the trajectory, one line per event, in the form
.B lucibridge -v
writes: the bytes sent to the model, the model's response, each tool
call and its result, and
.BI "turn done stop=" reason
at the end.  Ends as
.B text
does.
.TP
.BI id /status
read:
.BR idle ,
.BR working ,
.B blocked
(a call waits on
.BR approve ),
or the name of the tool running.
.TP
.BI id /approve
read: while a turn runs, waits until a call needs the operator's
approval, then gives
.IR "callid tool args" ;
when the session is idle it is empty.  Write
.BI allow " callid"
or
.BI deny " callid"
to answer.
.SH TOOL CALLS
The tool calls of one model response are a batch.  Each is first
gated, or intercepted
.RB ( say
is shown, not run), or answered from the read cache, in the model's
order.  What remains runs concurrently when every call is read-only
.RB ( read ,
.BR list ,
.BR find ,
.BR search ,
.BR grep ,
.BR websearch ,
.BR webfetch ,
.BR spawn ),
and one at a time, in order, when any call mutates, so a write precedes
the read or compile that follows it.  Every call is bounded to sixty
seconds.  Output too large for the model is written to
.I scratchdir
and summarised by its first lines.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; id=`{cat /mnt/veltro/new}
; echo 'what does nsconstruct.b do?' > /mnt/veltro/$id/input
; cat /mnt/veltro/$id/text
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/veltro/veltrosrv.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR veltro (1),
.IR llmsrv (4),
.IR tools9p (4),
.IR nsconstruct (2)
